//www.worldcargonews.com/news/2025/11/suez-canal-in-recovery-mode/","type":"secondary","access":"public","published":"2025-11","retrieved":"2026-09-03"},{"id":"worldoil-uk-nov2024","name":"UK sanctions Russian marine insurers Alfastrakhovanie and VSK","publisher":"World Oil","url":"https://www.worldoil.com/","type":"secondary","access":"public","published":"2024-11","retrieved":"2026-09-06"},{"id":"worldwideais-providers","name":"AIS Data Providers in 2026: Who's Independent, Who's Not","publisher":"Worldwide AIS Network","url":"https://www.worldwideais.org/post/ais-data-providers-in-2026-who-s-independent-who-s-not-and-why-it-matters","type":"secondary","access":"public","notes":"Interested party — it sells a competing decentralised AIS network. The consolidation timeline it gives is checkable against the acquisition announcements.","retrieved":"2026-09-03"},{"id":"wsj-floating-armories","name":"How Floating Armories Help Guard Cargo Ships From Pirates on High Seas","publisher":"The Wall Street Journal","url":"https://www.wsj.com/articles/how-floating-armories-help-guard-cargo-ships-from-pirates-on-high-seas-1422934573","type":"secondary","access":"paywalled","retrieved":"2026-09-03"}],"retrieved_note":"Every source carries a retrieved date: when this library last read it. Publisher figures can change after that date without notice.","retrieved":"2026-09-06","count":366},"stats-library":{"dataset":"stats-library","title":"Every number we hold, with its source","updated":"2026-09-04","description":"A flat index of the headline statistics across the whole library. Every entry carries the figure, the period it refers to, who published it, and whether it is a published figure or our own arithmetic. Derived figures are marked and their method is stated.","legend":{"published":"Stated in the cited source.","derived":"Our arithmetic on published inputs. Method given.","flagged":"Held but contradicted or unverified. Do not use externally."},"stats":[{"stat":"Merchant ships in the world fleet","value":"112,501","unit":"vessels, 100 GT and above","period":"1 Jan 2025","kind":"published","sources":[{"id":"unctad-rmt-2025","publisher":"UNCTAD","url":"https://unctad.org/system/files/official-document/rmt2025_en.pdf"}]},{"stat":"World fleet capacity","value":"2.44 billion","unit":"DWT","period":"1 Jan 2025","kind":"published","sources":[{"id":"unctad-rmt-2025","publisher":"UNCTAD","url":"https://unctad.org/system/files/official-document/rmt2025_en.pdf"}]},{"stat":"Global seaborne trade","value":"12,292","unit":"million tonnes","period":"2023","kind":"published","sources":[{"id":"unctad-rmt-2024","publisher":"UNCTAD","url":"https://unctad.org/system/files/official-document/rmt2024_en.pdf"}]},{"stat":"Container port throughput","value":"920","unit":"million TEU","period":"2024","kind":"published","note":"Port moves, double-counts transhipment.","sources":[{"id":"unctadstat-containers","publisher":"UNCTAD Data Hub","url":"https://unctadstat.unctad.org/insights/theme/109"}]},{"stat":"Fully cellular container ships","value":"6,822","unit":"ships","period":"3 Sep 2026","kind":"published","sources":[{"id":"alphaliner-top100","publisher":"Alphaliner / AXSMarine","url":"https://alphaliner.axsmarine.com/PublicTop100/"}]},{"stat":"Container fleet capacity","value":"34,220,569","unit":"TEU","period":"3 Sep 2026","kind":"published","sources":[{"id":"alphaliner-top100","publisher":"Alphaliner / AXSMarine","url":"https://alphaliner.axsmarine.com/PublicTop100/"}]},{"stat":"Average container ship size","value":"5,016","unit":"TEU","period":"3 Sep 2026","kind":"derived","method":"34,220,569 TEU / 6,822 ships","sources":[{"id":"alphaliner-top100","publisher":"Alphaliner / AXSMarine","url":"https://alphaliner.axsmarine.com/PublicTop100/"}]},{"stat":"Average fleet age","value":"12.5 by DWT, 22.4 by ship count","unit":"years","period":"2024","kind":"published","sources":[{"id":"unctad-rmt-2024-ch2","publisher":"UNCTAD","url":"https://unctad.org/system/files/official-document/rmt2024ch2_en.pdf"}]},{"stat":"Top 5 carrier share of container capacity","value":"65.7%","period":"3 Sep 2026","kind":"derived","method":"Sum of MSC, Maersk, CMA CGM, COSCO, Hapag-Lloyd shares","sources":[{"id":"alphaliner-top100","publisher":"Alphaliner / AXSMarine","url":"https://alphaliner.axsmarine.com/PublicTop100/"}]},{"stat":"Daily vessel operating cost","value":"$5,071 to $8,162","unit":"per day","period":"FY2024 to Q1 2026","kind":"published","note":"Bulk cheapest, tankers dearest.","sources":[{"id":"starbulk-q1-2026","publisher":"Star Bulk Carriers","url":"https://www.starbulk.com/media/uploads_file/2026/05/20/p1jp3g2m39dca9s41dsf1t6u1h8e4.pdf"},{"id":"scorpio-q3-2025","publisher":"Scorpio Tankers","url":"https://www.scorpiotankers.com/scorpio-tankers-inc-announces-financial-results-for-the-third-quarter-of-2025-and-an-increase-to-its-quarterly-dividend/"}]},{"stat":"Daily TCE, VLCC","value":"$74,200","unit":"per day","period":"Q4 2025","kind":"published","sources":[{"id":"frontline-q4-2025","publisher":"Frontline","url":"https://www.frontlineplc.cy/fro-fourth-quarter-and-full-year-2025-results/"}]},{"stat":"Daily TCE, Capesize","value":"$26,627","unit":"per day","period":"Q1 2026","kind":"published","sources":[{"id":"starbulk-q1-2026","publisher":"Star Bulk Carriers","url":"https://www.starbulk.com/media/uploads_file/2026/05/20/p1jp3g2m39dca9s41dsf1t6u1h8e4.pdf"}]},{"stat":"Daily TCE, Panamax","value":"$15,849","unit":"per day","period":"Q1 2026","kind":"published","sources":[{"id":"starbulk-q1-2026","publisher":"Star Bulk Carriers","url":"https://www.starbulk.com/media/uploads_file/2026/05/20/p1jp3g2m39dca9s41dsf1t6u1h8e4.pdf"}]},{"stat":"Container spot rate, Drewry WCI composite","value":"$4,465","unit":"per FEU","period":"3 Sep 2026","kind":"published","sources":[{"id":"drewry-wci","publisher":"Drewry","url":"https://www.drewry.co.uk/supply-chain-advisors/supply-chain-expertise/world-container-index-assessed-by-drewry"}]},{"stat":"Container spot rate, Freightos FBX composite","value":"$3,532","unit":"per FEU","period":"4 Sep 2026","kind":"published","note":"Differs from Drewry by 26%. Different baskets.","sources":[{"id":"freightos-fbx","publisher":"Freightos / Baltic Exchange","url":"https://www.freightos.com/enterprise/terminal/freightos-baltic-index-global-container-pricing-index/"}]},{"stat":"Realised revenue per TEU","value":"$1,492 to $1,549","unit":"per TEU","period":"FY2024","kind":"published","note":"About a third of headline spot per FEU.","sources":[{"id":"ing-container-2025","publisher":"ING Research","url":"https://think.ing.com/articles/container-shipping-sailing-through-uncertainty-and-looming-overcapacity/"}]},{"stat":"Cargo value per TEU","value":"$54,500","period":"2020","kind":"published","sources":[{"id":"pem-container-value","publisher":"Port Economics, Management and Policy","url":"https://porteconomicsmanagement.org/pemp/contents/part1/maritime-shipping-and-international-trade/value-containerized-trade/"}]},{"stat":"Cargo value on an average container ship","value":"$273 million","period":"2026 fleet, 2020 values","kind":"derived","method":"5,016 TEU x $54,500","sources":[{"id":"alphaliner-top100","publisher":"Alphaliner / AXSMarine","url":"https://alphaliner.axsmarine.com/PublicTop100/"},{"id":"pem-container-value","publisher":"Port Economics, Management and Policy","url":"https://porteconomicsmanagement.org/pemp/contents/part1/maritime-shipping-and-international-trade/value-containerized-trade/"}]},{"stat":"Bunker VLSFO","value":"$684.50 Rotterdam to $860.50 Singapore","unit":"per tonne","period":"4 Sep 2026","kind":"published","sources":[{"id":"shipandbunker-sin","publisher":"Ship & Bunker","url":"https://shipandbunker.com/prices/apac/sea/sg-sin-singapore"},{"id":"shipandbunker-rtm","publisher":"Ship & Bunker","url":"https://shipandbunker.com/prices/emea/nwe/nl-rtm-rotterdam"}]},{"stat":"Baltic Dry Index","value":"3,488","period":"3 Sep 2026","kind":"published","sources":[{"id":"hellenic-bdi","publisher":"Hellenic Shipping News","url":"https://www.hellenicshippingnews.com/baltic-dry-index-climbs-to-3488-up-157-points/"}]},{"stat":"Seafarer minimum wage, able seafarer","value":"$690","unit":"per month","period":"from 1 Jan 2026","kind":"published","sources":[{"id":"itf-wages","publisher":"ITF","url":"https://www.itfseafarers.org/en/resources/wages"}]},{"stat":"War risk premium, southern Red Sea","value":"0.3% to over 1% of hull value","unit":"per seven-day cover","period":"July 2026","kind":"published","sources":[{"id":"reuters-warrisk-2026-07","publisher":"Reuters","url":"https://www.reuters.com/world/middle-east/war-risk-insurance-costs-surge-southern-red-sea-voyages-after-houthi-attacks-2026-07-23/"}]},{"stat":"War risk premium, pre-campaign baseline","value":"0.03%","period":"before Dec 2023","kind":"published","sources":[{"id":"reuters-warrisk-2023-12","publisher":"Reuters","url":"https://www.reuters.com/world/middle-east/war-risk-insurance-rates-edge-up-after-surge-red-sea-ship-attacks-2023-12-04/"}]},{"stat":"War risk premium, Strait of Hormuz","value":"7.5-10% of hull value","period":"July 2026","kind":"flagged","note":"Contradicted by Reuters, which has Hormuz at 0.5%. Probably a units error. Do not use.","sources":[{"id":"reuters-warrisk-2025-06","publisher":"Reuters","url":"https://www.reuters.com/world/middle-east/escalating-hormuz-tensions-drive-up-middle-east-war-risk-insurance-costs-sources-2025-06-23/"}]},{"stat":"IMB piracy incidents","value":"445 (2010) falling to 116 (2024)","kind":"published","note":"Down 74%.","sources":[{"id":"imb-annual-reports","publisher":"ICC Commercial Crime Services","url":"https://www.icc-ccs.org/piracy-reporting-centre/imb-piracy-annual-reports"}]},{"stat":"Total ship losses","value":"127 (2010) falling to 27 (2024)","kind":"published","note":"Down 79%.","sources":[{"id":"allianz-safety","publisher":"Allianz Commercial","url":"https://commercial.allianz.com/news-and-insights/reports/shipping-and-safety-review.html"}]},{"stat":"Attack rate per transit, Red Sea 2024","value":"0.545%","kind":"derived","method":"72 attacks / 13,213 Suez transits. Suez is a proxy denominator.","sources":[{"id":"reuters-suez-2024","publisher":"Reuters","url":"https://www.reuters.com/world/middle-east/suez-canal-revenues-plunge-61-2024-2025-01-01/"}]},{"stat":"Expected loss per transit","value":"0.128% of hull, about $102,600 on an $80m ship","kind":"derived","method":"attack rate x 23.5% total-loss share from named incidents. Severity input is weak.","sources":[{"id":"reuters-suez-2024","publisher":"Reuters","url":"https://www.reuters.com/world/middle-east/suez-canal-revenues-plunge-61-2024-2025-01-01/"},{"id":"reuters-warrisk-2024-09","publisher":"Reuters","url":"https://www.reuters.com/world/middle-east/red-sea-insurance-costs-soar-houthi-shipping-threats-loom-sources-say-2024-09-19/"}]},{"stat":"Premium to expected loss ratio","value":"about 5x","kind":"derived","method":"0.65% midpoint premium / 0.128% expected loss","sources":[{"id":"reuters-warrisk-2026-07","publisher":"Reuters","url":"https://www.reuters.com/world/middle-east/war-risk-insurance-costs-surge-southern-red-sea-voyages-after-houthi-attacks-2026-07-23/"}]},{"stat":"Exposure window, Gulf of Aden IRTC","value":"490 nm, 33 hours at 15 kn to 49 hours at 10 kn","kind":"derived","method":"Published 490 nm corridor length divided by speed.","sources":[{"id":"imo-piracy","publisher":"IMO","url":"https://www.imo.org/en/OurWork/Security/Pages/PiracyArmedRobberydefault.aspx"}]},{"stat":"Suez transits","value":"26,434 (2023) falling to 13,213 (2024)","kind":"published","note":"Halved.","sources":[{"id":"reuters-suez-2024","publisher":"Reuters","url":"https://www.reuters.com/world/middle-east/suez-canal-revenues-plunge-61-2024-2025-01-01/"}]},{"stat":"Suez revenue","value":"$10.25bn (2023) falling to $3.99bn (2024)","kind":"published","note":"Down 61%.","sources":[{"id":"reuters-suez-2024","publisher":"Reuters","url":"https://www.reuters.com/world/middle-east/suez-canal-revenues-plunge-61-2024-2025-01-01/"}]},{"stat":"Cape diversion penalty","value":"+3,500 nm, +10 days, +40% fuel","kind":"published","sources":[{"id":"unctad-redsea-routes","publisher":"UNCTAD","url":"https://unctad.org/news/ships-face-longer-routes-higher-costs-and-emissions-red-sea-crisis"}]},{"stat":"Extra daily cost when diverting, largest boxships","value":"about $1 million per day","period":"Jan 2024","kind":"published","sources":[{"id":"reuters-divert-cost","publisher":"Reuters","url":"https://www.reuters.com/world/middle-east/shipping-firms-avoid-red-sea-suez-canal-after-us-strikes-yemen-2024-01-12/"}]},{"stat":"Marine security and USV companies catalogued","value":"123","kind":"published","note":"Our own dataset."},{"stat":"Companies scoring 4 of 4 on proximity","value":"1 (Seasats)","kind":"derived","method":"Computed from proximity axes."},{"stat":"Vessels under management, top five ship managers","value":"about 3,095","kind":"derived","method":"Sum of V.Group, Anglo-Eastern, BSM, Wilhelmsen, Columbia published fleets.","sources":[{"id":"lloyds-top-shipmanagers-2025","publisher":"Lloyd's List","url":"https://www.lloydslist.com/LL1155778/Top-10-shipmanagers-2025"},{"id":"marineinsight-shipmanagers","publisher":"Marine Insight","url":"https://www.marineinsight.com/top-10-ship-management-companies/"}]},{"stat":"Commercial purchases of autonomous escort on record","value":"0","kind":"published","note":"A deliberate search found none."}],"counts":{"total":38,"published":28,"derived":9,"flagged":1}},"tactics":{"$schema":"../schema/tactics.schema.json","dataset":"tactics","title":"How ships are actually attacked","description":"Four distinct threat models operate at sea, and they share almost nothing. Confusing them is the most common error in maritime security marketing: the countermeasure that stops a man with a ladder does nothing against a drone boat, and vice versa. Each model below is separated by attacker, approach, weapon, intent and what actually defeats it.","updated":"2026-09-02","threat_models":[{"id":"somali-pag","name":"Somali Pirate Action Group","intent":"Hijack the ship and hold ship and crew together for ransom","status_2026":"Actively resurgent. Attacking and seizing vessels at a rate not seen since 2011.","organisation":"Pirate Action Groups (PAGs) operating from motherships, enabling operations far offshore","platforms":[{"type":"Attack skiff","detail":"Small high-speed open boat, up to 25 knots. Usually two or more per attack."},{"type":"Open whaler","detail":"Carries significant fuel, often towing one or more attack skiffs."},{"type":"Mothership","detail":"Most commonly dhows, but also hijacked fishing vessels and merchant ships. Carries pirates, stores, fuel and skiffs. Where size allows, skiffs are carried aboard and camouflaged. The original crew is often held aboard as hostages."}],"range":"Motherships extend skiff range far into the Indian Ocean and are far less weather-limited. Historic reach beyond 1,000 nautical miles; the Hellas Aphrodite was boarded roughly 700 nautical miles off Somalia in November 2025.","approach":"From the quarter or the stern — the blind, low, slow part of the ship","timing":"Any time, day or night, but experience shows dawn and dusk are more likely","weapons":["Small arms","Rocket-propelled grenades"],"weapon_use":"Fired at the bridge and accommodation to intimidate the Master into slowing or stopping, not to sink the ship","boarding_method":"Long lightweight ladders, knotted climbing ropes, or long hooked poles up the ship's side. Once aboard they head for the bridge and demand the ship slows so the rest can board.","treatment_of_crew":"Crew normally kept aboard with the ship while ransom is negotiated. Occasionally separated by nationality and taken ashore. It is in the pirates' interest to keep captives alive, though intimidation and torture have occurred, and some captivities have lasted years.","vulnerability_factors":["Low freeboard","Low speed","Ocean transits far from response","No embarked security team"],"defeated_by":["Enhanced lookout and early detection","Speed and freeboard","Evasive manoeuvring","Razor wire and physical barriers","Water spray and foam monitors","Citadel","Armed security team","Naval response within range"],"source_ids":["bmp5","imb-h1-2026","lwj-2026-06-gulf-of-aden","gcaptain-hellas-aphrodite"],"confidence":"high"},{"id":"houthi-standoff","name":"Houthi stand-off attack","intent":"Damage or sink the ship, or deter the trade. Not to board and not to ransom.","status_2026":"Reduced from the 2024 peak but the capability and the risk classification remain","organisation":"State-adjacent armed group with a stated targeting criterion based on ownership, management or port-call affiliation","platforms":[{"type":"Anti-ship missile","detail":"Long range, accurate and powerful. Ships other than the intended target have been hit when the controller misidentified or the missile homed on the wrong return."},{"type":"One-way attack UAV","detail":"Used against the Eternity C among others."},{"type":"Uncrewed surface vessel","detail":"Remotely operated explosive boat. Sank the Tutor in June 2024."},{"type":"Manned assault boat","detail":"Small arms and RPGs from speed boats, as used against the Magic Seas."},{"type":"Helicopter","detail":"The Galaxy Leader was boarded from a Mil Mi-17 in November 2023 — the only such case."},{"type":"Sea mine","detail":"Used to deny access to Yemeni ports. Usually tethered, but can break free and drift into lanes. Merchant ships are not the target."}],"approach":"Stand-off. The attacker never needs to come alongside.","weapons":["Anti-ship missiles","UAVs","USVs","Small arms","RPGs","Sea mines"],"targeting_logic":"Affiliation, not opportunity. The Eternity C and Magic Seas were reportedly selected over prior Israeli port calls or ownership and ship-manager affiliations. A ship cannot harden its way out of who owns it.","vulnerability_factors":["Ownership or management affiliation","Prior port calls","Transit through Bab el-Mandeb within weapon range"],"defeated_by":["Routing outside weapon range","Naval air defence","Hard-kill or soft-kill counter-UAS and counter-USV"],"not_defeated_by":["Razor wire","Water cannon","Citadel","Armed guards on deck","Speed"],"note":"BMP itself says the difference matters: attacks by extremists may be more determined because the attackers may be willing to risk their lives, and against a water-borne IED the mitigation measures available to a merchant ship are explicitly limited.","source_ids":["bmp5","wikipedia-houthi-attacks","reuters-eternity-c-crew"],"confidence":"high"},{"id":"se-asia-robbery","name":"Southeast Asian armed robbery","intent":"Steal and leave. Not to take the ship, not to take the crew.","status_2026":"Sharply down after enforcement — 12 IMB-reported Singapore Straits boardings in H1 2026 against 57 in H1 2025","organisation":"Small opportunistic groups, typically two to five perpetrators, non-confrontational, hit-and-run","platforms":[{"type":"Small boat","detail":"Approaches from astern in a busy, restricted, slow-steaming lane."}],"approach":"From astern, mostly after dark, while the ship is underway in a traffic lane or at anchor","timing":"Mostly after dark","weapons":["Knives","Increasingly firearms — Singapore Straits gun reports rose from 8 in 2024 to 27 in 2025"],"what_they_take":"Engine spare parts, scrap metal, store contents. Perpetrators are frequently sighted in or near the engine room.","target_profile":"Larger, slower vessels. In the Malacca and Singapore Straits in H1 2026: 62% bulk carriers, 29% barges under tow, 9% tankers.","vulnerability_factors":["Slow steaming in restricted lanes","Low freeboard","Barges under tow","Unlit access points","Anchor watch lapses"],"defeated_by":["Visible countermeasures on deck","Deck lighting and randomised patrols","Securing store lockers and hawse pipes","Littoral law enforcement — two Indonesian Marine Police arrests in July 2025 cut the regional count by roughly three quarters"],"source_ids":["recaap-hy-2026","imb-annual-2025","imb-h1-2026"],"confidence":"high"},{"id":"gog-kfr","name":"Gulf of Guinea kidnap for ransom","intent":"Take senior crew ashore and ransom the people, not the ship","status_2026":"Heavily suppressed — only two IMB-reported incidents in H1 2026, against a historic position as the world's primary kidnapping hotspot","organisation":"Organised KFR groups operating from the Niger Delta","platforms":[{"type":"Fast boat","detail":"Capable of operating far offshore — one 2026-reported case saw six pirates board about 115 nautical miles southeast of Lomé."}],"approach":"Offshore interception, boarding, then withdrawal by fast boat with hostages","who_they_take":"Senior crew — Master and Chief Engineer — and any Western or foreign crew members. Hostages are normally taken ashore in the Niger Delta and ransomed.","severity":"In 2025 this region accounted for 23 of the world's 25 kidnapped crew across just four incidents.","defeated_by":["Regional naval presence","Nigeria's Deep Blue Project and coordinated regional initiatives","Citadel procedures that keep senior officers off the deck"],"reporting_body":"MDAT-GoG, operated by the French and UK navies. Note MDAT-GoG does not coordinate responses to vessels under attack — it is an information centre, not a rescue service.","source_ids":["imb-annual-2025","imb-h1-2026","marad-2026-009-gog"],"confidence":"high"}],"defence_layers":{"framework":"BMP: detect, avoid, deter, delay, report. The company and Master cannot influence an attacker's capability or intent — only the opportunity. Every measure below removes opportunity.","source_ids":["bmp5","bmp-maritime-security"],"layers":[{"layer":"Primary","measures":[{"name":"Lookout and vigilance","detail":"BMP calls an effective lookout the single most effective method of ship protection, because early detection is what makes every other measure usable. Elevated all-round watch, shortened rotations, anti-glare binoculars, thermal and night-vision optics, radar watch, bridge-wing anti-piracy mirrors, CCTV and fixed search lights covering the stern."},{"name":"Manoeuvring","detail":"Practised avoidance manoeuvres at best possible speed. BMP notes the hydrostatic pressure generated by a hard-manoeuvring ship can defeat a determined attack more effectively than speed alone."},{"name":"Speed and freeboard","detail":"Naval architecture as security. Boardability is a property of the hull before it is a property of the crew."},{"name":"PCASP","detail":"Privately Contracted Armed Security Personnel. See the economics dataset for what they cost."}]},{"layer":"Secondary","measures":[{"name":"Razor wire","detail":"High-tensile concertina, 730mm or 980mm coils, double roll where possible, every third ring fixed to the rails with a steel cable through the core so it cannot be pulled away. Quality varies enormously and cheap wire is much less effective."},{"name":"Water spray and foam monitors","detail":"Fixed, remotely operated — manual activation exposes the operator. Makes it hard for a boat to stay alongside and much harder to climb. Water cannons sweep a vertical arc; spray rails produce a curtain. Foam is disorienting and slippery but must be additional to firefighting stock."},{"name":"Door and access hardening","detail":"Limited, controlled access points. Motion sensors and CCTV."},{"name":"Alarms","detail":"Distinctive alarms backed by PA announcement, drilled so the time for all crew to reach safety is known. Continuous whistle can distract attackers."}]},{"layer":"Last","measures":[{"name":"Citadel or safe muster point","detail":"Internal hardening plus independent communications. The crew's fallback once the deck is lost."},{"name":"Hidden position transmitter","detail":"BMP advises companies fit one, because disabling visible communications and tracking aerials is among the first things hijackers do."},{"name":"Ship Security Alert System","detail":"Tested before entry, with an emergency communication plan and prepared messages posted at every external comms station including the citadel."}]}],"operational_practice":["Avoid drifting, waiting, anchoring and slow steaming inside the transit corridor — 15 of the 32 successful attacks in H1 2026 happened while the vessel was anchored.","Keep AIS on so militaries can track the ship, but restrict the broadcast to identity, position, course, speed and navigational status.","Reschedule planned maintenance on voyage-critical equipment away from the high-risk transit, and confirm the plant can make full speed before entry.","Control information: keep correspondence with agents, charterers and chandlers minimal, particularly about rendezvous points and waiting positions. Voyage detail leaks.","Minimise VHF use inside the area and answer only known callers — impostors are possible.","Brief and drill the crew before entering, not during an attack, including the different actions required for a piracy attack versus a stand-off attack."]},"transit_corridors":[{"id":"mstc","name":"Maritime Security Transit Corridor","detail":"A military-established corridor where naval forces concentrate presence and surveillance. Comprises the Internationally Recommended Transit Corridor, the Bab el-Mandeb Traffic Separation Scheme, the TSS west of the Hanish Islands, and a two-way route connecting the IRTC to the BAM TSS. Shown on maritime security chart UKHO Q6099.","source_ids":["bmp5"]},{"id":"irtc","name":"Internationally Recommended Transit Corridor","detail":"Not a traffic separation scheme but an established corridor in the Gulf of Aden where naval forces focus counter-piracy patrols. Group transits and national convoys may be offered within it.","source_ids":["bmp5"]},{"id":"hra","name":"High Risk Area","detail":"An industry-defined area inside the UKMTO Voluntary Reporting Area where a higher risk of attack is considered to exist and additional security measures may be needed. Outlined on chart Q6099.","source_ids":["bmp5"]},{"id":"vra","name":"Voluntary Reporting Area","detail":"The UKMTO area within which ships are encouraged to register, establishing direct contact between ship and UKMTO.","source_ids":["bmp5"]}],"retrieved":"2026-09-02"},"threat-stats":{"$schema":"../schema/threat-stats.schema.json","dataset":"threat-stats","title":"Reported incident counts","description":"Piracy and armed robbery counts from the three bodies that publish them. The three disagree, on purpose — they count different waters and take reports from different people. Never mix them in one series.","updated":"2026-09-02","counting_bodies":[{"id":"IMB","name":"ICC International Maritime Bureau","scope":"worldwide","counts":"actual and attempted piracy and armed robbery reported to its Kuala Lumpur centre by masters and owners","source_id":"imb-prc"},{"id":"ReCAAP","name":"ReCAAP Information Sharing Centre","scope":"Asia only, 21 contracting parties","counts":"reports routed through member government focal points","source_id":"recaap-annual-2025"},{"id":"IMO","name":"International Maritime Organization","scope":"worldwide","counts":"reports from member governments plus regional and industry bodies; the widest net and the highest numbers","source_id":"imo-msc4-270"}],"series":[{"id":"imb-global-annual","body":"IMB","metric":"incidents against ships, worldwide","unit":"incidents","period":"calendar year","points":[{"period":"2023","value":120,"source_id":"imb-annual-2025","confidence":"high"},{"period":"2024","value":116,"source_id":"imb-annual-2025","confidence":"high"},{"period":"2025","value":137,"source_id":"imb-annual-2025","confidence":"high","note":"Highest in five years."}]},{"id":"imo-global-annual","body":"IMO","metric":"piracy and armed robbery incidents, worldwide","unit":"incidents","period":"calendar year","points":[{"period":"2025","value":171,"source_id":"imo-msc4-270","confidence":"medium","note":"Higher than IMB's 137 for the same year because IMO aggregates government reports too. A worked example of why the counting body must always travel with the number."}]},{"id":"recaap-asia-annual","body":"ReCAAP","metric":"piracy and armed robbery incidents, Asia","unit":"incidents","period":"calendar year","points":[{"period":"2024","value":107,"source_id":"recaap-annual-2025","confidence":"high"},{"period":"2025","value":132,"source_id":"recaap-annual-2025","confidence":"medium","note":"+23% year on year. ReCAAP's own website dashboard showed 133 for 2025; the annual report figure of 132 is used here. A one-incident discrepancy of this kind is normal and is why records get revised."}]},{"id":"recaap-asia-halfyear","body":"ReCAAP","metric":"armed robbery against ships, Asia","unit":"incidents","period":"January–June","points":[{"period":"2019-H1","value":28,"source_id":"recaap-hy-2026","confidence":"high"},{"period":"2025-H1","value":96,"source_id":"recaap-hy-2026","confidence":"high"},{"period":"2026-H1","value":35,"source_id":"recaap-hy-2026","confidence":"high","note":"Down 64%. Lowest January–June total since 2019. Every incident was armed robbery inside a coastal state's jurisdiction — none met the legal definition of piracy. 21 occurred while the ship was underway, 14 at anchor."}]},{"id":"soms-halfyear","body":"ReCAAP","metric":"incidents in the Straits of Malacca and Singapore","unit":"incidents","period":"January–June","points":[{"period":"2025-H1","value":80,"source_id":"recaap-hy-2026","confidence":"high"},{"period":"2026-H1","value":21,"source_id":"recaap-hy-2026","confidence":"high","note":"Down 74%, but still 60% of everything reported in Asia. Residual cases are petty theft localised to the eastbound lane of the Phillip Channel."}]},{"id":"singapore-strait-imb-annual","body":"IMB","metric":"incidents in the Singapore Straits","unit":"incidents","period":"calendar year","points":[{"period":"2024","value":43,"source_id":"imb-annual-2025","confidence":"high"},{"period":"2025","value":80,"source_id":"imb-annual-2025","confidence":"high","note":"58% of all incidents reported worldwide in 2025. IMB counts the Singapore Straits; ReCAAP counts the wider Malacca-and-Singapore corridor and recorded 108 for the same year — the highest in the 19 years from 2007. Same water, different boundary, different number."}]},{"id":"gulf-of-guinea-annual","body":"IMB","metric":"incidents in the Gulf of Guinea","unit":"incidents","period":"calendar year","points":[{"period":"2023","value":22,"source_id":"imb-annual-2025","confidence":"high"},{"period":"2024","value":18,"source_id":"imb-annual-2025","confidence":"high"},{"period":"2025","value":21,"source_id":"imb-annual-2025","confidence":"high","note":"Low count, high severity. 23 of the world's 25 kidnapped crew in 2025 were taken here, in four incidents."}]},{"id":"indonesia-archipelago-annual","body":"IMB","metric":"incidents within the Indonesian archipelago","unit":"incidents","period":"calendar year","points":[{"period":"2023","value":18,"source_id":"imb-annual-2025","confidence":"high"},{"period":"2024","value":22,"source_id":"imb-annual-2025","confidence":"high"},{"period":"2025","value":12,"source_id":"imb-annual-2025","confidence":"high"}]},{"id":"imb-global-halfyear","body":"IMB","metric":"incidents against ships worldwide, first half","unit":"incidents","period":"January-June","points":[{"period":"2025-H1","value":90,"source_id":"imb-h1-2026","confidence":"medium"},{"period":"2026-H1","value":38,"source_id":"imb-h1-2026","confidence":"medium","note":"Lowest first-half figure since 1992 — and the most misleading number in this library. Perpetrators still boarded or hijacked in 84% of those 38 incidents, 67 crew were taken hostage, and Somali groups accounted for 94% of hostages worldwide. Volume fell; consequence did not."}]},{"id":"singapore-strait-imb-halfyear","body":"IMB","metric":"incidents in the Singapore Straits, first half","unit":"incidents","period":"January-June","points":[{"period":"2025-H1","value":57,"source_id":"imb-h1-2026","confidence":"medium"},{"period":"2026-H1","value":12,"source_id":"imb-h1-2026","confidence":"medium","note":"Attributed to intelligence sharing and coordinated interdictions by the Indonesian Marine Police."}]},{"id":"imb-global-quarterly","body":"IMB","metric":"incidents against ships worldwide, first quarter","unit":"incidents","period":"January-March","points":[{"period":"2026-Q1","value":16,"source_id":"imb-q1-2026","confidence":"high","note":"14 boarded, one hijacked, one attempted. Two crew taken hostage and one injured."}]}],"breakdowns":[{"id":"imb-2025-outcomes","title":"What happened to the ship, IMB 2025","period":"2025","body":"IMB","source_id":"imb-annual-2025","total":137,"items":[{"label":"Boarded","value":121},{"label":"Attempted attack","value":10},{"label":"Hijacked","value":4},{"label":"Fired upon","value":2}],"note":"88% of incidents are boardings. The dominant threat is a small number of men coming over the rail at low speed, not a missile."},{"id":"imb-crew-harm","title":"What happened to the crew","body":"IMB","source_id":"imb-annual-2025","series":[{"2023":73,"2024":126,"2025":46,"label":"Taken hostage"},{"2023":14,"2024":12,"2025":25,"label":"Kidnapped"},{"2025":10,"label":"Threatened"},{"2025":4,"label":"Injured"},{"2025":3,"label":"Assaulted"}]},{"id":"imb-weapons","title":"Guns and knives reported, IMB","body":"IMB","series":[{"2024":26,"2025":42,"label":"Guns"},{"2024":39,"2025":33,"label":"Knives"}],"note":"IMB reported guns in 42 incidents in 2025 against 26 in 2024, and knives in 33 against 39. Rocket-propelled grenades, anti-ship missiles, uncrewed surface vessels and one-way attack UAVs are documented elsewhere in this library — in tactics.json under each threat model, in the named incidents, and in the UKMTO entries in attack-log.json — but they do not appear in this IMB tally. RPGs specifically are recorded by BMP5 as standard Somali pirate equipment, were used against the Hellas Aphrodite in November 2025, and were fired at a tanker in the Gulf of Aden on 15 June 2026.","scope":"The IMB annual summary breaks out guns and knives only. It is not a full weapons tally.","source_ids":["imb-annual-2025","bmp5","gcaptain-hellas-aphrodite","lwj-2026-06-gulf-of-aden"]},{"id":"imb-2025-by-region","title":"Where, IMB 2025","period":"2025","body":"IMB","source_id":"imb-annual-2025","items":[{"label":"Southeast Asia","value":95},{"label":"Africa","value":29},{"label":"Indian sub-continent","value":8}]},{"id":"soms-2026h1-ship-types","title":"Which ships were hit in the Malacca and Singapore Straits, H1 2026","period":"2026-H1","body":"ReCAAP","source_id":"recaap-hy-2026","unit":"percent of 21 incidents","items":[{"label":"Bulk carriers","value":62},{"label":"Barges under tow","value":29},{"label":"Tankers","value":9}],"note":"Slow, low-freeboard, often under tow. Boardability is a naval-architecture property before it is a security one."},{"id":"recaap-2026h1-by-country","title":"Asia H1 2026 vs H1 2025, by location","body":"ReCAAP","source_id":"safety4sea-recaap-hy2026","items":[{"label":"Straits of Malacca & Singapore","2025-H1":80,"2026-H1":21,"flag_iso":"SG"},{"label":"Philippines","2025-H1":0,"2026-H1":10,"flag_iso":"PH"},{"label":"Bangladesh","2025-H1":3,"2026-H1":2,"flag_iso":"BD"},{"label":"Indonesia (excl. SOMS)","2025-H1":6,"2026-H1":1,"flag_iso":"ID"},{"label":"Malaysia","2025-H1":0,"2026-H1":1,"flag_iso":"MY"},{"label":"India","2025-H1":3,"2026-H1":0,"flag_iso":"IN"},{"label":"South China Sea","2025-H1":2,"2026-H1":0,"flag_iso":null},{"label":"Vietnam","2025-H1":2,"2026-H1":0,"flag_iso":"VN"}],"note":"The Philippines went from zero to ten while everywhere else fell. Risk moves; it does not evaporate."},{"id":"imb-2026h1-outcomes","title":"What happened to the ship, IMB H1 2026","period":"2026-H1","body":"IMB","source_id":"imb-h1-2026","total":38,"items":[{"label":"Boarded","value":27},{"label":"Hijacked","value":5},{"label":"Fired upon","value":3},{"label":"Attempted","value":3}],"note":"Hijackings rose from 4 across all of 2025 to 5 in six months. The rarest and worst outcome is the one that increased."},{"id":"imb-2026h1-targets","title":"What was targeted, IMB H1 2026","period":"2026-H1","body":"IMB","source_id":"imb-h1-2026","items":[{"label":"Dry bulk carriers","value":13},{"label":"Container vessels","value":10}],"note":"Together 60% of targeted tonnage. Bulk carriers lead in every region and every dataset here — slow, low freeboard, small crews."},{"id":"imb-2026q1-status","title":"Outcome by vessel status when attacked, IMB Q1 2026","period":"2026-Q1","body":"IMB","source_ids":["imb-q1-2026"],"total":16,"items":[{"label":"Steaming","value":10},{"label":"Anchored","value":6}],"note":"Of the 10 while steaming: one attempted, eight boarded, one hijacked. All six anchored incidents were boardings. IMB notes most successful incidents were reported while vessels were steaming — the reverse of the H1 2026 picture, where 15 of 32 successful attacks were at anchor."},{"id":"imb-2026q1-region","title":"Incidents by region, IMB Q1 2026","period":"2026-Q1","body":"IMB","source_ids":["imb-q1-2026"],"total":16,"items":[{"label":"Southeast Asia","value":12},{"label":"Africa (Somalia)","value":2},{"label":"Africa (Gulf of Guinea)","value":1},{"label":"Rest of the world","value":1}],"note":"Southeast Asia was 12 of 16, all boardings. The two Somali incidents were one attempted attack and one hijacking of a dhow, which IMB notes could be used as a mother vessel."}],"findings":[{"id":"enforcement-works-and-that-is-the-problem","claim":"Incident counts respond to enforcement within a single quarter.","evidence":"Indonesian Marine Police apprehended two gangs in July 2025. IMB records a significant decline in Singapore Straits incidents across the second half of 2025, and ReCAAP records SOMS incidents falling 74% year on year in H1 2026.","source_ids":["imb-annual-2025","recaap-hy-2026"]},{"id":"somali-capability-persists","claim":"Somali piracy is suppressed by naval presence, not eliminated.","evidence":"Only a small number of incidents off Somalia in 2025, but two in November occurred far from shore, demonstrating the ability to still operate at range. IMB attributes the absence of a resurgence to sustained naval presence, vessel hardening and best management practices.","source_ids":["imb-annual-2025"]},{"id":"severity-not-frequency","claim":"The frequency trend and the severity trend point in opposite directions.","evidence":"Global incidents rose to a five-year high in 2025 while hostage-taking fell from 126 to 46; meanwhile kidnapping doubled to 25 and gun reports rose from 26 to 42.","source_ids":["imb-annual-2025"]},{"id":"reporting-lag","claim":"The dataset itself is late.","evidence":"The IMB Piracy Reporting Centre publicly raised concern about late reporting in its 2025 annual report, noting timely reporting supports preventive action.","source_ids":["imb-annual-2025"]},{"id":"somali-resurgence-2026","claim":"Somali piracy is no longer suppressed. It is resurgent, and this supersedes the 2025 reading.","evidence":"Since late April 2026, Somali pirates have been attacking and seizing vessels at a rate not seen since 2011. UKMTO issued four warnings in the Somali Basin in April and eight in the Gulf of Aden across May and June. Three hijacked vessels were being held simultaneously as of 16 June 2026. In H1 2026 Somali groups accounted for 94% of all crew taken hostage worldwide, and hijack victims included a Palau-flagged product tanker, a St Kitts and Nevis general cargo ship and multiple dhows taken for use as motherships.","source_ids":["lwj-2026-06-gulf-of-aden","imb-h1-2026"]},{"id":"low-counts-hide-high-success","claim":"Falling incident counts do not mean falling risk, because the success rate is rising.","evidence":"H1 2026 produced the lowest half-year count since 1992 at 38 incidents, yet perpetrators successfully boarded or hijacked in 32 of them — 84%. 67 crew were taken hostage from those 38 incidents, against 46 across all 137 incidents of 2025.","source_ids":["imb-h1-2026","imb-annual-2025"]},{"id":"anchorages-are-the-exposure","claim":"Ships are being lost at anchor, not only under way.","evidence":"15 of the 32 successful attacks in H1 2026 happened while the vessel was anchored. A Togo-flagged product tanker was boarded by nine armed men at anchor off Yemen and sailed with its 12 crew to the Somali coast. Manila anchorage went from zero incidents in H1 2025 to eight.","source_ids":["imb-h1-2026"]},{"id":"singapore-straits-q1-collapse","claim":"The Singapore Straits remain the world's largest single concentration of incidents while falling sharply.","evidence":"IMB recorded eight incidents in the Singapore Straits in Q1 2026 against 31 in the same quarter of 2025. It still accounted for the largest share reported globally.","source_ids":["imb-q1-2026"]},{"id":"philippines-anchorages-q1","claim":"Philippine anchorages went from zero to four incidents in one year.","evidence":"Four incidents in Q1 2026 — two at Manila anchorage and two at Bauan anchorage in Batangas Bay — against none in Q1 2025. One crew member was taken hostage.","source_ids":["imb-q1-2026"]}],"retrieved":"2026-09-02"},"vc-diligence":{"dataset":"vc-diligence","title":"What a VC would ask, and what we can actually answer","updated":"2026-09-04","description":"Every question a seed or Series A investor would put to us, scored by how well the library can answer it today. Strength is strong, weak or none. The questions we cannot answer are the point of this document.","market_sizing":{"anchor":"The war risk premium already being paid is the cleanest proxy for budget in the room.","calc":[{"metric":"Suez-route transits","value":13213,"period":"2024","kind":"published","source_ids":["reuters-suez-2024"]},{"metric":"Same, pre-crisis","value":26434,"period":"2023","kind":"published","source_ids":["reuters-suez-2024"]},{"metric":"War risk premium","value":"0.3% to 1.0% of hull per seven-day cover","period":"July 2026","kind":"published","source_ids":["reuters-warrisk-2026-07"]},{"metric":"Assumed average hull value","value":"$50 million","kind":"assumption","warning":"NOT SOURCED. We hold exactly one hull value, an $80m press estimate for the Sounion. This assumption drives the whole TAM and must be replaced."},{"metric":"Implied war risk spend on that route","value":"about $4.3 billion a year at 2024 volumes, $8.6 billion at 2023 volumes","kind":"derived","method":"transits x 0.65% midpoint premium x $50m assumed hull"},{"metric":"Premium per transit","value":"$325,000 on a $50m hull, $520,000 on an $80m hull","kind":"derived"}],"honest_caveat":"This is a spend pool, not a serviceable market. Nobody has ever bought autonomous escort, so our obtainable share of it is zero today and unproven at any level."},"questions":[{"theme":"Market","items":[{"question":"How big is the market?","why_they_ask":"They need a path to a large outcome.","our_answer_today":"About $4.3bn a year of war risk premium is paid on Suez-route transits at 2024 volumes, $8.6bn at 2023 volumes. That is the budget we compete with, not our revenue.","strength":"weak","evidence":"Derived from published transits and premiums, but rests on an unsourced $50m average hull value.","what_kills_us":"They will push on the hull assumption immediately, and we have one data point."},{"question":"Is the problem growing?","why_they_ask":"Growth markets forgive execution risk.","our_answer_today":"Wrong question, and we can now show why. Piracy fell because protection worked. NATO attributes the decline to naval presence, BMP and embarked armed security teams. IMB says the same in its 2026 report. The World Bank studied navies and armed guards as the two measures behind the Somali collapse. The timing fits: operations began 2008-2009, armed guards were permitted on UK ships in October 2011, and the count fell from 445 in 2010 to 296 in 2012. We are proposing the intervention that already worked, without the warship.","strength":"strong","evidence":"why-piracy-fell.json. NATO, IMB, World Bank, ETH Zurich and UK Government all on the record.","what_kills_us":"It also proves the incumbent solutions work, which invites the question of why anyone needs a new one. The answer must be cost and availability, not efficacy. And navies are finite: the resurgence in 2023-2026 coincides with attention shifting elsewhere."},{"question":"Why now?","why_they_ask":"Timing thesis.","our_answer_today":"Suez transits halved and canal revenue fell 61% in one year. War risk went from 0.03% to over 1% of hull. Uncrewed platforms became commercially available.","strength":"strong","evidence":"reuters-suez-2024, the Reuters premium series, market-players.json.","what_kills_us":"'Why now' cuts both ways: if the Red Sea calms, the premium and the urgency both vanish."}]},{"theme":"Problem","items":[{"question":"What is the actual chance a ship gets attacked?","why_they_ask":"They will do this arithmetic themselves.","our_answer_today":"About 0.55% per transit in the Red Sea in 2024, expected loss around 0.128% of hull. But the sharper number is scope: across 6,595 recorded attacks from 1993 to 2020, 59.6% happened to ships at anchor, berthed or moored. Only 40.4% were under way. An escort addresses the 40%.","strength":"weak","evidence":"risk-quantification.json and attack-data-validation.json, cross-checked against Crime at Sea.","what_kills_us":"Any market sizing built on total incident counts is overstated by more than half. We should present the 40% figure ourselves rather than have it found."},{"question":"Why is the premium five times expected loss?","why_they_ask":"Tests whether we understand our own market.","our_answer_today":"Underwriters price uncertainty, scarcity and everything beyond hull loss. That gap is where a risk-reduction product earns.","strength":"strong","evidence":"risk-quantification.json with both sides argued.","what_kills_us":"If they think the gap is just underwriter conservatism, they will expect it to close."},{"question":"How long is a ship actually exposed?","why_they_ask":"Sizes the engineering problem.","our_answer_today":"The Gulf of Aden corridor is 490 nm and 2 nm wide. A ship is inside for 33 hours at 15 knots, 49 at 10.","strength":"strong","evidence":"imo-piracy. Bounded corridor with published lanes.","what_kills_us":"We have no equivalent figure for Bab el-Mandeb, where the Houthi attacks actually happen."}]},{"theme":"Product","items":[{"question":"Can you actually build it?","why_they_ask":"Technical risk.","our_answer_today":"Unknown to me. Nothing in this library records our platform, its cost, or its endurance.","strength":"none","evidence":"Nothing.","what_kills_us":"This is an internal gap, not a research one. It must be filled before any pitch."},{"question":"Can your platform hold merchant speed for two days?","why_they_ask":"The corridor demands it.","our_answer_today":"The requirement is known: 33 to 49 hours at 10 to 15 knots. Whether we meet it is not recorded.","strength":"none","evidence":"Requirement derived from imo-piracy.","what_kills_us":"Platform research shows fast platforms have hours of endurance and persistent ones cruise at 2 to 5 knots, below merchant speed. This is the central engineering tension in the whole category."},{"question":"What happens when it is attacked?","why_they_ask":"Escort that dies first is worthless.","our_answer_today":"Not recorded.","strength":"none","evidence":"Nothing.","what_kills_us":""}]},{"theme":"Competition","items":[{"question":"Who else does this?","why_they_ask":"Standard.","our_answer_today":"123 companies catalogued. One scores 4 of 4 on our proximity model, Seasats, whose site names HVU escort. BlackSea scores 3 of 4 and is being acquired by AEVEX.","strength":"strong","evidence":"market-players.json, two full dossiers.","what_kills_us":"Seasats raised $20m led by Konvoy with $40m total and holds $89m and $24m Navy contracts. They are ahead on capital and customers."},{"question":"Why has nobody done this?","why_they_ask":"The killer question.","our_answer_today":"59 companies build USVs. Every commercial-only one does survey or ocean data, not security. The military builders do not sell to shipowners.","strength":"strong","evidence":"market-players.json, commercial-demand-evidence.json.","what_kills_us":"The honest reading is that it may be unbuilt because the demand is not there, not because nobody thought of it."},{"question":"What stops Anduril or Saronic doing this tomorrow?","why_they_ask":"Defensibility.","our_answer_today":"Focus, and a different business. They sell platforms to navies. We sell a service to shipowners. Of 123 companies catalogued, 59 build uncrewed surface vessels and 23 deliver protection as a service, but only 2 do both, and only 1 does both for commercial customers. The service providers deliver with crewed boats and armed teams; the USV builders sell hardware to defence programmes. Nobody is running an uncrewed protection service for merchant shipping.","strength":"weak","evidence":"market-players.json proximity axes. escort means delivering protection alongside a client vessel rather than selling a component; uncrewed means building the platform; commercial means serving non-defence buyers.","what_kills_us":"Focus is a real advantage but it is not a moat. A defence prime could stand up a service arm. The honest version is that the barrier is business model and customer relationship, not technology, and business model barriers fall when a market is proven. Our defensibility has to come from being first to the shipowner relationship, not from anything they cannot build.","the_positioning":"We are not a vessel manufacturer. We sell Marine Security as a Service. That distinction decides who we hire, what we publish, how we price, and which customers we chase."}]},{"theme":"Business model","items":[{"question":"What do you charge?","why_they_ask":"Unit economics.","our_answer_today":"No price is set. The only escort pricing that exists anywhere is from 2012: about $30,000 for one boat covering four vessels for three to four days.","strength":"none","evidence":"route-economics.json, low confidence, fourteen years old.","what_kills_us":"We cannot answer the most basic commercial question."},{"question":"What is the customer's alternative?","why_they_ask":"Anchors your price ceiling.","our_answer_today":"Three: reroute via the Cape at +3,500 nm, +10 days and +40% fuel; embarked armed guards; or accept the premium. About $1m a day extra for the largest boxships on the Cape route.","strength":"strong","evidence":"unctad-redsea-routes, reuters-divert-cost.","what_kills_us":"The Cape is a real, working substitute that needs no vendor."},{"question":"What are the unit economics?","why_they_ask":"Margin.","our_answer_today":"Not recorded. We know what ships cost to run, $5,071 to $8,162 a day, but nothing about our own platform.","strength":"none","evidence":"vessel-economics.json for their side only.","what_kills_us":""}]},{"theme":"Traction","items":[{"question":"Who wants this?","why_they_ask":"The only question that really matters at seed.","our_answer_today":"Nobody yet. Zero customers, zero letters of intent, zero pilots recorded.","strength":"none","evidence":"commercial-demand-evidence.json.","what_kills_us":"A deliberate search found no commercial purchase of autonomous escort by anyone, ever. We are creating the category."},{"question":"Have you talked to customers?","why_they_ask":"Evidence of demand discovery.","our_answer_today":"Not yet. We hold 33 target accounts, 18 named contacts and a written sequence, but nothing has been sent.","strength":"weak","evidence":"crm/accounts.json, crm/contacts.json.","what_kills_us":"The strongest asset we have is a DPA/CSO first-degree connection. One conversation would change this answer."},{"question":"What did the last people to try this learn?","why_they_ask":"Pattern matching.","our_answer_today":"Convoy Escort Programme ran armed patrol boats escorting convoys in 2012. We do not know what happened to it.","strength":"none","evidence":"route-economics.json escort_precedent.","what_kills_us":"Not knowing why the only precedent stopped is a bad look in a diligence call."}]},{"theme":"Risk","items":[{"question":"What if the Red Sea calms down?","why_they_ask":"Concentration risk.","our_answer_today":"The premium collapses and the budget with it. Bab el-Mandeb was 0.03% before December 2023 and over 1% in July 2026.","strength":"weak","evidence":"Reuters premium series.","what_kills_us":"We have no answer for a peacetime scenario. Somali piracy resurgence is a partial hedge, not a plan."},{"question":"Regulatory position?","why_they_ask":"Can you deploy.","our_answer_today":"Deliberately parked. Not researched.","strength":"none","evidence":"questions.json, parked domain.","what_kills_us":"Four open questions on uncrewed escort legality, none started."}]}],"the_five_that_hurt":["Nobody has ever bought this. Zero transactions on record anywhere.","Only about 40% of recorded attacks happen to a ship under way. The rest are at anchor or berthed, where an escort does nothing.","We have no price, because no current market price for escort exists.","Per transit, the attack probability is about half a percent. A rational owner may decline.","Focus is a positioning advantage, not a structural moat. Nothing stops a platform builder adding a service arm."],"what_we_would_answer_well":["Why piracy fell. Protection worked, on the record from NATO, IMB and the World Bank, and the timing fits. That turns our weakest question into our strongest.","The integrity of our own numbers. The IMB series cross-checks against an independent geocoded dataset to within six incidents a year across eleven years.","The threat picture, sourced to incident level, with the ownership chains behind attacked vessels.","The competitive map: 123 companies, scored, with the whitespace identified and two full dossiers.","The economics of the alternative: Cape reroute, armed guards, and the premium being paid.","The exposure window, which turns a vague mission into a bounded 490 nm, 33 to 49 hour engineering spec.","That we know exactly what we do not know. The question bank is 32 questions with status on each."],"cheapest_things_that_would_change_the_answer":["One conversation with a DPA or CSO. We have a first-degree connection who holds that role.","One broker call for a current security price and a real hull value distribution.","One underwriter conversation on whether escort earns a premium discount.","Finding out what happened to the Convoy Escort Programme."],"moat":{"claim":"Focus, and selling a service rather than vessels.","evidence":{"companies_catalogued":123,"build_usvs":59,"deliver_protection_as_a_service":23,"do_both":2,"do_both_for_commercial_buyers":1,"service_providers_using_crew_and_boats":21,"usv_builders_selling_hardware_only":57},"reading":"The market splits cleanly. Service providers protect ships using crewed boats and armed men. Platform builders sell uncrewed hardware to navies. The crossing point is nearly empty.","honest_limit":"This is a positioning advantage, not a structural moat. Nothing stops a platform builder adding a service arm. What we can defend is the customer relationship, if we get there first."}},"world-land":{"projection":"equirectangular","viewBox":[0,0,2000,1000],"note":"Natural Earth 110m land, Ramer-Douglas-Peucker simplified. Public domain. Generated by tools/build-world-path.py.","paths":["M669.0,944.7 665.8,950.0 641.7,949.6 631.7,945.9 656.2,946.6 663.3,942.4 669.0,944.7Z","M115.5,941.7 104.8,942.4 97.6,940.5 90.5,936.6 93.9,934.6 104.2,935.4 114.0,939.1 115.5,941.7Z","M749.1,933.6 756.0,936.0 758.4,939.4 759.3,944.6 719.5,950.1 706.4,949.8 699.1,948.0 700.1,945.7 711.9,944.2 716.7,942.3 729.7,933.6 740.8,932.4 749.1,933.6Z","M326.6,908.3 333.8,909.2 340